	affiliations = {University of Mohaghegh Ardabili, Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering, Ardabil, Iran},
	abstract = {The rapid growth in Electric Bikes (EBs) as a preferred mode of urban transportation necessitates innovative approaches to Power Distribution Network Expansion Planning (PDN-EP). This study introduces a novel Coordinated Expansion Planning (CEP) model for efficiently deploying Electric Bike Urban Charging Stations (EB-UCSs) integrated with renewable energy sources, reactive power support devices, and grid reinforcement strategies. The proposed model utilizes a robust hybrid methodology combining Probability & Statistics (PS), Stochastic Programming (SP), and Chance-Constrained Programming (CCP) to effectively address uncertainties in EB demand behavior, conventional load demand, Distributed Generation (DG) power output, and Substation Capacity Optimization (SCO). By applying the model to both 54-bus and 123-bus test systems, the study evaluates the operational and economic performance of PDNs under deterministic and non-deterministic scenarios. Results reveal that integrating EB-UCSs within the PDN significantly reduces grid reinforcement costs and enhances system reliability and sustainability. In the 54-bus system, the CEP framework achieves cost reductions of 3.51 %, 5.21 %, and 4.69 % in deterministic (Case I and II) and non-deterministic conditions (Case IV) compared to the most challenging scenario (Case III). Similarly, in the 123-bus system, cost savings of 3.39 %, 9.71 %, and 4.17 % are observed for the same cases. Strategic placement of EB-UCSs near wind turbines (WTs) and capacitor banks (CBs) minimizes transmission losses and optimizes renewable energy utilization. Including these infrastructures also ensures voltage stability and reduces substation overloading, thereby fostering resilient and sustainable urban energy systems. Furthermore, the proposed classical mathematical approach demonstrates superior performance in terms of cost efficiency and computational time compared to metaheuristic algorithms, such as the Whale Optimization Algorithm (WOA), Grey Wolf Optimization (GWO), and Ant Lion Optimizer (ALO). For instance, in the 123-bus system under Case IV, the classical approach achieves a computational time of 2.9 h, significantly lower than the 5.8–7.2 h required by metaheuristic methods. This highlights the scalability and practical feasibility of the proposed model for large-scale applications. The study's findings underscore the transformative potential of integrating EB-UCSs into PDNs. They offer a blueprint for policymakers and planners to design low-carbon, cost-effective, and resilient urban energy systems. The proposed CEP framework aligns with sustainable development goals by promoting renewable energy integration, reducing greenhouse gas emissions, and accelerating the adoption of electrified transportation systems. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
